## Setup

```{r setup}
library(tidyverse)
library(peopleanalyticsdata)
library(brms)
library(tidybayes)
library(ggdist)

theme_set(theme_minimal(base_size = 13))
set.seed(2026)

data("salespeople", package = "peopleanalyticsdata")

# Chapter 1 showed this dataset has a few gaps in `sales`; drop_na()
# here keeps the rest of this chapter's code simple.
salespeople <- salespeople |>
  drop_na(sales, customer_rate, performance, promoted) |>
  mutate(
    customer_rate_s = as.numeric(scale(customer_rate)),
    performance_f = factor(performance)
  )
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Back to our opening question

*What predicts who gets promoted?* We asked this in Chapter 1 and have
been building toward it ever since.

The outcome `promoted` is **0 or 1** — and that needs a different kind
of model.

### Why a straight line fails

If we fit an ordinary regression to a 0/1 outcome, the line keeps
going.

::: callout-important
Extend it far enough and it predicts a **−0.3 probability** of
promotion, or a **1.4**. Both are nonsense — probabilities live between
0 and 1. We need something that bends.
:::

### The fix: model the log-odds

$$
\text{promoted}_i \sim \text{Bernoulli}(p_i), \qquad
\text{logit}(p_i) = \alpha + \beta_1 \text{customer\_rate}_i + \beta_2 \text{performance}_i
$$

### One word changes in `brms`

```{r}
#| eval: false
brm(sales ~ customer_rate, family = gaussian())               # continuous outcome
brm(promoted ~ customer_rate_s + performance_f, family = bernoulli())  # yes/no outcome
```

Priors, posteriors, credible intervals, checks — all identical to what
you already know.

A prior on the log-odds scale is hard to eyeball directly — `1.5`
doesn't mean much until you translate it. As always, look before you
fit: simulate draws from the intercept prior and translate them to a
probability:

```{r plot-prior-logit}
set.seed(9)

tibble(logit_intercept = rnorm(4000, 0, 1.5)) |>
  mutate(prob = plogis(logit_intercept)) |>
  ggplot(aes(prob)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 40, fill = "#122a52", alpha = 0.6) +
  labs(title = "What Normal(0, 1.5) on the log-odds scale implies",
       subtitle = "Simulated draws from the intercept prior, translated to a baseline promotion probability",
       x = "Implied baseline promotion probability", y = "Simulated draws")
```

::: callout-note
Spread fairly evenly across plausible promotion rates, with a little
extra weight near the extremes — reasonable for "we don't know the
baseline rate yet," and nowhere near committing to, say, "everyone
gets promoted."
:::

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Fit the model

```{r fit-logit}
fit_logit <- brm(
  promoted ~ customer_rate_s + performance_f,
  data = salespeople, family = bernoulli(),
  prior = c(prior(normal(0, 1.5), class = Intercept),
            prior(normal(0, 1),   class = b)),
  chains = 4, iter = 2000, seed = 9, refresh = 0
)

summary(fit_logit)
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Interpreting the results

### Translation 1 — odds ratios

**Odds** = the chance it happens divided by the chance it doesn't. An
**odds ratio** says how that multiplies.

```{r odds-ratios}
fixef(fit_logit) |>
  as_tibble(rownames = "term") |>
  mutate(odds_ratio = exp(Estimate)) |>
  select(term, Estimate, odds_ratio)
```

An odds ratio above 1 means *more* likely to be promoted.

### Translation 2 — predicted probabilities

Odds ratios are exact but abstract. **Probabilities** are what a
manager can act on.

```{r predicted-prob}
grid <- expand_grid(
  customer_rate_s = seq(-2, 2, by = 0.1),
  performance_f = levels(salespeople$performance_f)
)

grid |>
  add_epred_draws(fit_logit) |>
  mean_qi(.epred, .width = 0.95) |>
  ggplot(aes(customer_rate_s, .epred, colour = performance_f, fill = performance_f)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = .lower, ymax = .upper), alpha = 0.15, colour = NA) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c("#8fabd0", "#4a6fa5", "#d32f2f", "#122a52")) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("#8fabd0", "#4a6fa5", "#d32f2f", "#122a52")) +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent) +
  labs(title = "Predicted promotion probability",
       subtitle = "Rises with customer rating, and with performance tier",
       x = "Customer rating (standardised)", y = "P(promoted)", colour = NULL, fill = NULL)
```

::: callout-important
## This chart is the deliverable

Promotion likelihood **climbs with customer rating** and is
**dramatically higher for top performers** — with honest uncertainty
bands around both. A talent team could act on this tomorrow: check
whether the promotion process is weighting these factors the way
leadership intends.
:::

### Does it fit?

```{r ppc}
pp_check(fit_logit, type = "bars", ndraws = 100) +
  labs(title = "Posterior predictive check (binary outcome)")
```

